WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.767
Last clip, every answer had a source.

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:06.167
This clip, every rule behind
the engines gets a signature.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:07.867
A rule that changes who you contact

00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:09.967
should never rest on
one person's judgment.

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:14.300
Here it can't. By the end you'll take
a definition from draft to signed,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.033
with two people on the record.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.700
Leo tightens a suppression rule
in the rush before quarter end.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.033
By the forecast call, Mia's best
accounts are quietly excluded,

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:25.933
and nobody can say who changed it or when.

00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:28.967
Nothing reaches an engine
until two different

00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:31.333
people have put their name to it.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.600
First, what a definition is.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.167
It's a piece of your go to market
written down so the system can read it:

00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.900
who you target, how you score,
what your brand may say,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.500
who is never contacted. Definitions
as code means three things.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.533
They're scoped to your
organization, versioned, and signed.

00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:49.200
Engines read them at run time, and
only the signed version counts.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.767
An unsigned draft is ignored, and
the engine falls back to its default.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.167
The kinds, in plain terms. Segments
and personas say who you sell to.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:01:01.800
Scoring models and the buying
committee model say who fits.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.333
Brand voice, suppression rules and a
compliance policy say what you may say,

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:08.867
and to whom. Lanes, sequences
and plays say how outreach runs.

00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:12.333
Journeys, stage maps and money
say what your numbers mean.

00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:14.267
The generated reference in your docs

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.600
folder lists every kind with its fields.

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:18.500
Second, the draft, in a ChangeSet:

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.400
a bundle of proposed changes
reviewed as one unit.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.533
In the console, edit a definition
on its settings page, like Journeys,

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:28.533
Money or Proof library,
or in Environments.

00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:30.100
From the command line, write the files,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.200
then run gtm plan to preview the
difference against what's live.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.133
Plan writes nothing.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:36.000
Third, approval.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.733
Run gtm apply with the environment
dev, and it proposes the ChangeSet.

00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:40.733
Nothing changes yet.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:44.467
A person who isn't the proposer
opens the approval card in Approvals.

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:47.433
The impact preview says what
would change and for whom.

00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:51.333
Then they approve or reject. The agent
that drafted it can never approve it,

00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:53.833
and neither can the person
that agent works for.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.567
Fourth, the signature. An approved
ChangeSet puts the definition in dev,

00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:00.767
but engines still don't trust
it. Propose it for signing,

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.933
in Environments or with gtm
definition propose for signing.

00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:04.800
Then a person signs.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.600
The signer can't be the proposer, nor
the person behind a proposing agent.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.767
Pro move: gtm definition sign
all, with the environment dev,

00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:15.200
signs everything proposed at
once. Signing is human only.

00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:16.933
Here's the same flow with a team.

00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:21.167
Leo drafts the stricter rule and
runs plan. Orbit computes the impact.

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:26.133
Sophia reads the card, sees the accounts
it would suppress, and approves.

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:30.133
Leo proposes it for signing, and Mia
signs, because she didn't propose it.

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:31.867
No one person does every step.

00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:36.100
Why two people? A rule that changes
who you contact is a business decision.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.367
Every change is a new version, so
you can see what changed, when,

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:43.067
and who signed it. Every step
is on the audit log. To go back,

00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:46.700
gtm rollback proposes returning a
definition to an earlier version.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.033
That proposal needs a second person too.

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:53.333
Or ask your agent: "Draft a stricter
suppression rule for competitors,

00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:56.533
validate it, show the impact
preview and propose it.

00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:00.767
Do not approve or sign anything".
It reports the approval and stops,

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:03.767
because approving and
signing aren't its to do.

00:03:03.833 --> 00:03:06.367
Fifth, two checks keep it honest.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:10.333
Environments shows a banner when
definitions sit in dev and aren't live.

00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:15.200
And gtm check compares environments
and exits non zero on drift,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.400
so it works as a gate in C I.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.500
Or ask your agent: "What
is in dev and not live".

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:21.700
One boundary to know.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.133
Environments are named places:
dev, staging and production.

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:28.667
A change lands in the
environment it was proposed for,

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:30.400
not the one you're viewing.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.233
Real customer facing effects outside
production are recorded, not sent.

00:03:34.233 --> 00:03:38.200
Promoting and rolling back is the
clip called Environments: dev,

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.400
staging and production.

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:43.400
The objection: two people on every
rule change will slow us down.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.067
It's two people per rule, not per message.

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:49.500
A draft sits harmlessly in dev, because
engines ignore it until it's signed.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.567
Sign all clears a batch in one command.

00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:55.533
Your definitions, signed once,
and every engine follows them.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.500
Keep these: Draft and preview the
impact. A different person approves.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.567
A person who didn't propose it signs.

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:04.533
Nothing reaches an engine
until two different

00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:06.867
people have put their name to it.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.500
You can now take any rule
from idea to signed config,

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.033
with every step on the record. Run gtm
check on your own organization today.

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:20.833
Next up: your ICP and scoring model,
the first definition worth drafting.
